        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 902,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1016,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Draws straight lines between the points of a table and reads values off them. The table is a set of positions (xp) and the values at them (fp); for each x you send, t.interp finds where it falls among the positions and blends the two nearest values in proportion.\n\nUse it as a breakpoint curve - a control that rises fast, levels off and rises again, drawn with a few points - or as a lookup table, a transfer function, a calibration from raw sensor readings to real units. Send it many x at once to resample at any positions, not only evenly spaced ones: a movement recorded with uneven timestamps read off at steady frames, or played back along a tempo curve (time-warping) - x is then the moment in the recording to read at each output frame. A table of poses (one row per position) gives back whole poses.\n\nt.interpolate resizes by an even factor; t.interp reads wherever you ask. The work is done on x's device.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.interp\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nx:\nWhere to read the table: a number, list or tensor of any shape. Every input is looked up and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nxp:\nThe positions of the table's points. They are held; send them first. If they are not in increasing order they are sorted (with their values) for you. If xp is never sent, the positions are 0, 1, 2 ... - the index of each value - so x 1.5 reads halfway between the second and third values.\n\nfp:\nThe values at those positions, held. One per position. A 2-D fp - one row per position, with several columns - is read column by column, so a table of xyz points gives back xyz points.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nvalues:\nThe value read at each x, the shape of x (plus fp's columns). Below the first position the first value is given, and above the last the last: the curve is held flat beyond its ends.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: a curve with three points: at 0 it is 0, at 0.25 it has already reached 0.875, and at 1 it is 1. 64 positions from 0 to 1 read through it rise steeply then level off - the shape of the table, not a straight line.\n\nBottom: with no xp, the table 0 10 30 is indexed by position. 0.5 is halfway between 0 and 10, so 5; 1.5 halfway between 10 and 30, so 20; 2 and beyond are 30.\n\nRELATED:\nt.interpolate resamples by an even factor. np.interp is the numpy version. lerp blends between two values by a fraction; t.linspace makes evenly spaced positions.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Draws straight lines between the points of a table and reads values off them. The table is a set of positions (xp) and the values at them (fp); for each x you send, t.interp finds where it falls among the positions and blends the two nearest values in proportion.\n\nUse it as a breakpoint curve - a control that rises fast, levels off and rises again, drawn with a few points - or as a lookup table, a transfer function, a calibration from raw sensor readings to real units. Send it many x at once to resample at any positions, not only evenly spaced ones: a movement recorded with uneven timestamps read off at steady frames, or played back along a tempo curve (time-warping) - x is then the moment in the recording to read at each output frame. A table of poses (one row per position) gives back whole poses.\n\nt.interpolate resizes by an even factor; t.interp reads wherever you ask. The work is done on x's device.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.interp\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nx:\nWhere to read the table: a number, list or tensor of any shape. Every input is looked up and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nxp:\nThe positions of the table's points. They are held; send them first. If they are not in increasing order they are sorted (with their values) for you. If xp is never sent, the positions are 0, 1, 2 ... - the index of each value - so x 1.5 reads halfway between the second and third values.\n\nfp:\nThe values at those positions, held. One per position. A 2-D fp - one row per position, with several columns - is read column by column, so a table of xyz points gives back xyz points.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nvalues:\nThe value read at each x, the shape of x (plus fp's columns). Below the first position the first value is given, and above the last the last: the curve is held flat beyond its ends.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: a curve with three points: at 0 it is 0, at 0.25 it has already reached 0.875, and at 1 it is 1. 64 positions from 0 to 1 read through it rise steeply then level off - the shape of the table, not a straight line.\n\nBottom: with no xp, the table 0 10 30 is indexed by position. 0.5 is halfway between 0 and 10, so 5; 1.5 halfway between 10 and 30, so 20; 2 and beyond are 30.\n\nRELATED:\nt.interpolate resamples by an even factor. np.interp is the numpy version. lerp blends between two values by a fraction; t.linspace makes evenly spaced positions."
        },
